"Diffusion and Osmosis: Random Walks, Osmotic Pressure and Red Cells | VirtualDemos",
"Diffusion and Osmosis: Random Walks, Osmotic Pressure and Red Cells",
"Student worksheet · College Physics 2e",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>College Physics 2e</em>, §{#} (molecular transport phenomena: diffusion, osmosis, and related processes); the same section in <em>College Physics for AP Courses 2e</em>. Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/college-physics-2e.",
"<strong>Used here:</strong> the <strong>Diffusion</strong>, <strong>Osmosis in a U tube</strong> and <strong>Red blood cell</strong> modes, chosen in the bar above the picture. The Capillary mode is not used.",
"The bar above the picture holds <strong>Play</strong>, <strong>Reset</strong>, the pace ({#}/{#} pace, Normal pace, {#} times the pace) and the four modes. Under each mode, <strong>Setups</strong> lists ready made experiments; the <strong>Set up</strong> card on the picture holds that mode’s sliders. Click a slider and use the arrow keys to move it one step at a time.",
"<strong>Diffusion, From a thin line:</strong> simulated molecules start on the center line and each takes its own random walk. The clock under the title says how much time has passed and how much one second on the screen stands for. The readout <strong>Spread now, simulated x_rms</strong> is the root mean square distance of the molecules from the line, worked out from the molecules themselves.",
"<strong>Osmosis in a U tube:</strong> two arms of water joined at the bottom by a membrane that lets water through but not the solute. Each arm starts filled {#} cm above the membrane. The <strong>Piston pushing on the right</strong> slider (steps of {#} kPa) pushes down on the right arm. The readout <strong>Water now flows</strong> says which way water is crossing the membrane at this moment.",
"<strong>Red blood cell:</strong> red cells under a microscope in salt water. The <strong>Salt outside</strong> slider (grams of NaCl per {#} mL, steps of {#}) and the chips under it set the solution; Play lets the cells take up or lose water until they stop. The readouts give the outside concentration in mOsm/L, the cell’s <strong>Volume now</strong> and, after Play, its <strong>Fate</strong>.",
"<strong>Record reading</strong> (under Readings) stores the present state. <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> sends the readings to the Data tables below: spreading readings go to Table {#}, U tube readings to Table {#} and red cell readings to Table {#}. <strong>Clear</strong> starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of its table.",
"In the Lab fold, <strong>Blind: hide predictions and answers</strong> hides the readouts that give away what you are measuring.",
"<strong>What the demo models.</strong> Diffusion: each molecule takes a random step at every short time step, which is exactly diffusion with the constant \\(D\\) of Table {#} ({#} °C, {#} atm); the molecules are simulated, so every run is a little different. U tube: van ’t Hoff’s law for dilute, ideal solutions, with \\(R\\) = {#} J/(mol·K), water taken as {#} kg/m³ and \\(g\\) = {#} m/s². Red cell: the cell gains or loses water until the particles inside and outside are equally concentrated; {#} % of its volume is hemoglobin and other solids that take no part (a model value), and its membrane keeps its area of {#} µm².",
"Measure how fast molecules spread by diffusion and find the diffusion constant \\(D\\), measure the osmotic pressure of sugar and salt solutions and find \\(R\\) from it, and find how a red blood cell’s volume depends on the salt around it.",
"<strong>Diffusion is a random walk.</strong> A molecule in a liquid is hit billions of times a second by its neighbors, so it moves in a zigzag with no preferred direction. Start many molecules on one line and they spread out on both sides. Their typical distance from the line along one direction, the root mean square distance \\(x_\\text{rms}\\), grows as the square root of the time (§{#}):",
"\\[x_\\text{rms} = \\sqrt{2Dt}.\\]",
"\\(D\\), the diffusion constant, depends on the molecule and what it moves through (Table {#}). Squaring both sides,",
"\\[x_\\text{rms}^2 = 2D\\,t,\\]",
"so a graph of \\(x_\\text{rms}^2\\) against \\(t\\) is a straight line through the origin with slope \\(2D\\). Going twice as far takes four times as long. That is why diffusion is fast across a cell (a few micrometers) and slow across a cup of tea.",
"<strong>Osmosis.</strong> A <em>semipermeable</em> membrane lets water through but holds back larger dissolved molecules (the solute). If pure water is on one side and a solution on the other, water crosses into the solution: water moves toward the side with more dissolved particles. This flow can be stopped by pushing on the solution. The pressure that just stops it is the <strong>osmotic pressure</strong> \\(\\Pi\\). For a dilute solution, van ’t Hoff found",
"\\[\\Pi = i\\,c\\,R\\,T,\\]",
"where \\(c\\) is the concentration in moles per cubic meter ({#} mmol/L = {#} mol/m³), \\(T\\) is the temperature in kelvins, \\(R\\) = {#} J/(mol·K) is the gas constant, and \\(i\\) is the number of particles each unit of solute makes in water: \\(i\\) = {#} for sugar, which stays whole, and \\(i\\) = {#} for table salt, NaCl, which splits into Na⁺ and Cl⁻. Osmotic pressure counts particles, not mass. With solute on both sides, only the difference in concentration counts. Pushing on the solution harder than \\(\\Pi\\) drives water out of it, through the membrane: <strong>reverse osmosis</strong>, the way drinking water is made from seawater.",
"In a U tube with no piston, water crosses until the solution side stands higher than the water side. The extra height \\(\\Delta h\\) pushes back with a pressure \\(\\rho g \\Delta h\\), and the flow stops when that equals the osmotic pressure.",
"<strong>A red cell is an osmometer.</strong> The particles inside a red blood cell cannot cross its membrane, but water can. Concentration is often given as <em>osmolarity</em>, the moles of dissolved particles per liter, in mOsm/L: {#} % saline ({#} g of NaCl in {#} mL) has the same osmolarity as the inside of a red cell, and is called <em>isotonic</em>. In a weaker (hypotonic) solution water flows in and the cell swells; in a stronger (hypertonic) one water flows out and the cell shrinks. Water moves until the inside is as concentrated as the outside. Since the number of particles inside stays the same, the water part of the cell’s volume is inversely proportional to the outside concentration \\(c\\). The rest, the hemoglobin and other solids, keeps its volume \\(V_s\\) whatever the solution:",
"\\[V = V_s + \\frac{k}{c},\\]",
"where \\(k\\) is a constant for the cell. This is the Boyle and van ’t Hoff relation, used in biology labs to find the solid volume of cells: a graph of \\(V\\) against \\(1/c\\) is a straight line whose intercept is \\(V_s\\). A red cell’s membrane hardly stretches, so the cell can swell only until it becomes a sphere with the same surface area. Beyond that it bursts (hemolysis).",
"Glucose in water has \\(D = 6.7\\times10^{-10}\\) m²/s. Find \\(x_\\text{rms}\\) after {#} s, {#} s and {#} s, in micrometers. By what factor does the spread grow each time the time is multiplied by {#}?",
"Find the osmotic pressure of {#} mmol/L sugar water (\\(i\\) = {#}) at {#} °C, in kPa, and the height of a column of water ({#} kg/m³) that would press with the same pressure.",
"Show that {#} % saline ({#} g of NaCl, {#} g/mol, in {#} mL) is about {#} mOsm/L. (Find the moles of NaCl per liter, then count two particles for each.)",
